is the coordinate vector of x (relative to B), or the B−coordinate vector of x. The mapping $\mathbf{x}\mapsto\begin{bmatrix}
      \mathbf{x}
      \end{bmatrix}_{B}$ is the coordinate mapping (determined by B﻿).1

example.paragraph:
Consider a basis $B = \left\{ \mathbf{b}_{1}\text{,~}\mathbf{b}_{2} \right\}$ for $\text{ℝ}^{2}$, where $\mathbf{b}_{1} = \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      0
      \end{bmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{b}_{2} = \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}$. Suppose an $\mathbf{x}$ in $\text{ℝ}^{2}$ has the coordinate vector $\text{[}\mathbf{x}_{B}\text{]} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Find $\mathbf{x}$.

Let $\mathbf{b}_{1} = \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}$, $\mathbf{b}_{2} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $x = \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \\
      5
      \end{bmatrix}$, and $B = \left\{ \mathbf{b}_{1}\text{,~}\mathbf{b}_{2} \right\}$. Find the coordinate vector $\left\lbrack \mathbf{x} \right\rbrack_{B}$ of $\mathbf{x}$ relative to $B$.

paragraph.math:
$$\left\lbrack \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{w} \right\rbrack_{B} = \begin{bmatrix}
      c_{1} & + & d_{1} \\
       & \vdots & \\
      c_{n} & + & d_{n}
      \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
      c_{1} \\
       \vdots \\
      c_{n}
      \end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix}
      d_{1} \\
       \vdots \\
      d_{n}
      \end{bmatrix} = \left\lbrack \mathbf{u} \right\rbrack_{B} + \left\lbrack \mathbf{w} \right\rbrack_{B}$$

statement.paragraph:
The vectors $\mathbf{v}_{1} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{v}_{2} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      {- 8}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{v}_{3} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 3} \\
      7
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ span $\text{ℝ}^{2}$ but do not form a basis. Find two different ways to express $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ as a linear combination of $\mathbf{v}_{1}$, $\mathbf{v}_{2}$, $\mathbf{v}_{3}$.

Let $B = \left\{ {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\text{,~}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      9
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \right\}$. Since the coordinate mapping determined by $B$ is a linear transformation from ${\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ into ${\mathbb{R}}^{2}$, this mapping must be implemented by some $2 \times 2$ matrix $A$. Find it. [Hint:Multiplication by $A$ should transform a vector $\mathbf{x}$ into its coordinate vector $\mathbf{\lbrack x\rbrack}_{B}$.]

Show that a subset $\left\{ \mathbf{u}_{1}\text{,}\text{…}\text{,~}\mathbf{u}_{p} \right\}$ in $V$ is linearly independent if and only if the set of coordinate vectors $\left\{ \left\lbrack \mathbf{u}_{1} \right\rbrack_{B}\text{,}\text{…}\text{,\,}\left\lbrack \mathbf{u}_{p} \right\rbrack_{B} \right\}$ is linearly independent in $\mathbf{R}^{n}$. [Hint: Since the coordinate mapping is one-to-one, the following equations have the same solutions, $c_{1}\text{,}\text{…}\text{,~}c_{p}$.] $$\begin{matrix}
      {c_{1}\mathbf{u}_{1} + \cdots + c_{p}\mathbf{u}_{p}} & = & 0 & {\mspace{6mu}\text{The~zero~vector~in}~V} \\
      \left\lbrack {c_{1}\mathbf{u}_{1} + \cdots + c_{p}\mathbf{u}_{p}} \right\rbrack_{B} & = & \lbrack 0\rbrack_{B} & {\mspace{6mu}\text{The~zero~vector~in}~\text{ℝ}^{n}}
      \end{matrix}$$

One of the octahedral sites is $\begin{bmatrix}
      {1\text{/}2} \\
      {1\text{/}4} \\
      {1\text{/}6}
      \end{bmatrix}\,,$ relative to the lattice basis. Determine the coordinates of this site relative to the standard basis of $\text{ℝ}^{3}$.

One of the tetrahedral sites is $\begin{bmatrix}
      {1\text{/}2} \\
      {1\text{/}2} \\
      {1\text{/}3}
      \end{bmatrix}\,.$ Determine the coordinates of this site relative to the standard basis of ${\mathbb{R}}^{3}$.
